Binary Digits picoCTF 2026

Challenge Name: Binary Digits
Difficulty: Easy
Category: Forensics
Author: Yonathan

I was given a .bin file with no hints, so the only thing I had to work with was digits.bin.

Step 1: Identify the File

I first ran the file command to check what type of file it was:

$ file digits.bin
digits.bin: ASCII text, with very long lines (65536), with no line terminators


This was the first hint. The file was simply ASCII text containing a very long sequence of 0s and 1s.

After using cat on the file, I confirmed that it consisted entirely of binary digits. It appeared that the original raw data had been expanded by a factor of n.

Step 2: Shrink the Data

To recover the original data, I used a Python script to shrink the binary sequence back to its original size. The script is available in the challenge files repository.

After running the script, I obtained:

decode.bin

Step 3: Identify the Recovered File

I then ran file on the new file:

$ file decode.bin
decode.bin: JPEG image data, JFIF standard 1.01, aspect ratio, density 1x1, segment length 16, baseline, precision 8, 800x500, components 3


At this point, the JPEG file immediately caught my attention.

Step 4: Recover the Flag

Since decode.bin was identified as JPEG image data, I renamed it from:

decode.bin


to:

decode.jpg


I then opened the image, and it contained the flag.

Flag: picoCTF{...}
